1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:08,699
This is the artist. It turns your results into figures, reports and slides that make the science clear.

2
00:00:09,099 --> 00:00:23,199
It draws publication figures, from model-accuracy curves to heatmaps and volcano plots, each one labelled, with proper axes and legends. It builds slide decks, and keeps a project report of the figures so far.

3
00:00:23,199 --> 00:00:32,500
What makes it different: it draws each figure from your real data and saves it at print resolution.

4
00:00:32,899 --> 00:00:44,600
The artist covers figures, reports and slides. The blacksmith computes the numbers, the bookworm supplies the references for captions, and the adversary checks that a figure is honest.

5
00:00:44,600 --> 00:00:53,900
It never invents a data point to fill a plot; when an input is missing, it says so.

6
00:00:54,299 --> 00:01:04,000
A real run, on simulated data. The member asks for one figure: colony size by growth medium, every point shown, the means marked.

7
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:16,500
The answer is Rendered. The image itself says the data are simulated, the caption says what to notice, and the artist states that it ran no test.

8
00:01:16,900 --> 00:01:28,599
Say what you want and where it is going: a figure sized for a journal, a deck for lab meeting, or a new page in the project report.

9
00:01:28,599 --> 00:01:38,500
Name your colours and figure size in the request; within a project it keeps one palette for every figure.

10
00:01:38,900 --> 00:01:53,799
To use it, type to Claude Code and name it: ask the artist for a figure from your results, and say what it is for. A figure for a talk differs from one for a paper.

11
00:01:53,799 --> 00:02:04,400
The reply starts with Rendered, Skipped or Failed. Then the figure, and a caption that says what to notice.
